  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/gymnorhamphichthys_cf_hypostomus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The sand knifefishes (Rhamphichthyidae) from South America belong to the weak electric fishes. They continuously send electric impulses which are used for tracing prey, orientation and intraspecific communication. Currently three genera and 14 species are known.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/gymnorhamphichthys_cf_hypostomus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The imported fish came from Peru. So far, no species of Gymnorhamphichthys is reported in the Checklist of the freshwater fishes of South America for Peru.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/gymnorhamphichthys_cf_hypostomus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The genus Gymnorhamphichthys, also called thermometer knifefishes, contains five described species. There is hardly anything known about the fish, because they are strictly nocturnal and stay buried in the sand during daytime. Thus comprehensive studies in the field are impossible.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/gymnorhamphichthys_cf_hypostomus_en/",
      "html": "<p>They are easy to feed, for they readily accept frozen bloodworms, which also form a larger part of their natural diet, as was shown in the field for Gymnorhamphichthys when the gut was dissected.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/gymnorhamphichthys_cf_hypostomus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish are very peaceful towards each other. They have no teeth at all nor any other feature that would enable them to hurt each other.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/gymnorhamphichthys_cf_hypostomus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The pattern of these specimens fits best the pattern described for G. hypostomus. This species, however, has a shorter snout. So it is also possible that these fish represent a species new to science, and they were therefore stocked under the name G. cf. hypostomus. Maximum size reported for G. hypostomus is about 20 cm; the specimens are 14–18 cm long.</p>"
    }
  ],
